speechtext:
There is one question on which I should like to have some general information, and I know the parliamentary assistant will be kind enough to explain it. As I understand it, when this convention was signed it was before Newfoundland came into confederation and Newfoundland signed as a contracting government on its own. There are certain obligations which the convention allots to each of the contracting governments. As an example article XI, section 3, subsection (a) states:
From the administrative budget there shall be deducted a sum of 500 United States dollars for each contracting government;
And that sort of thing. It is based on the assumption that Newfoundland and Canada were two separate contracting parties. Now that they have become one country, how has this been resolved? In other words, do we take over two shares of the responsibility, one for the original nine provinces and another for Newfoundland? Or do we merely absorb the obligations which were originally contracted for by Newfoundland into our own as one contracting party?
